## 1. Where you are, who else is on the machine, what you never touch

- You start in `{{BASE}}`, the base worktree of the GitHub repo `{{REPO}}` on
  `{{BASE_BRANCH}}`. `{{REPO}}` is an owner/name string for `gh` ONLY, never a
  path; every directory in this brief is `{{BASE}}` or `{{NIGHT_DIR}}`.
  `{{BASE}}` is a BASE ONLY. You do not develop in it. Every story gets its own
  worktree under `{{NIGHT_DIR}}/wt/<id>/` (section 3).
- The night run's own state lives in `{{NIGHT_DIR}}`: `queue.txt`,
  `state-{{RUN_DATE}}.txt`, `worktrees.tsv`, `logs/`, `run.lock`, `STOP`. The
  state file is PER RUN: `state.txt` is kept as a symlink to tonight's
  `state-{{RUN_DATE}}.txt`, so the old name still resolves, and a real
  `state.txt` left by an older runner was moved aside as
  `state-before-{{RUN_DATE}}.txt`, never deleted. One queue line can have MORE
  THAN ONE row there (a deferred row from the first pass and a terminal row
  from the second), and the LAST row for an id is its outcome. You do NOT write
  those rows: the runner appends every state row itself, and your output is
  captured into `logs/<id>.log` for you. `state.txt`, `queue.txt` and the BRIEF
  are denied to you by the permission layer, and that refusal is expected —
  never route around it. Your deliverables go to
  `{{BASE}}/runtime/AUTOPILOT-REPORT.md` and your own handoff file (section 9);
  the only file here you append to is `worktrees.tsv` (section 3), and you never
  delete `run.lock` or `STOP`.
- This is a SHARED machine running several unrelated services and, possibly,
  other Claude sessions. Before any port, container, service or reverse-proxy
  change: read `~/infra/INVENTORY.md` and `~/infra/PORTS.md`. Never stop a
  container that does not belong to {{PROJECT}}. If you change machine state,
  log it with `update-monitor note "..."` in English.
- Never run `git` in `{{BASE}}` for anything except `fetch`, `worktree`,
  `branch` and `tag`: if it is also a deployed tree, developing in it can ship
  untested code to a live service. Worktrees are created FROM it, never in it.
- Never touch a worktree this story did not create — another story, session or
  project may be living in it, with uncommitted work in it.
- Never merge, rebase, close or comment on a PR you did not open.
- Never edit `.github/**`, CI workflow files, secrets, credentials, `.env`
  files, or anything the project rules in section 6 list as forbidden. If a
  story appears to require a change there, that story is BLOCKED and you do not
  do it anyway: say so in the PR body, the report and the handoff, and exit with
  `RESULT <id> blocked PR#<n or -> review=parked rounds=<n> reason=forbidden`.
- Never install or enable a systemd unit, timer, cron entry or service on the
  machine. Writing unit files INTO the repo is fine; activating them is the
  owner's, in daylight.

