## 3. Per-story recipe

### 3.1 Worktree — decide the case BEFORE running a command

Paths and names are fixed:

    worktree   {{NIGHT_DIR}}/wt/<id>/
    branch     feat/{{BRANCH_PREFIX}}-<id>-<slug>        slug <= 5 words, lowercase, hyphenated

Always `git fetch` first, from `{{BASE}}` (the checkout on disk):

```bash
/usr/bin/git -C "{{BASE}}" fetch origin --prune
```

`git worktree add -b` exits non-zero when the branch already exists, so DECIDE
THE CASE FIRST, with these three checks, and only then run a `worktree` command.
Never run a command and react to its failure:

**Quote every path and branch name containing `<id>` or `<slug>`** — here and
everywhere else in this brief, always `"…/wt/<id>"`, never bare, and in DOUBLE
quotes (an apostrophe breaks a single-quoted string). Unquoted, an id with a
space or an apostrophe splits the command or leaves the quote unterminated, and
the story dies on its first line.

```bash
# 1 does the tree already exist?
test -d "{{NIGHT_DIR}}/wt/<id>" && echo TREE_EXISTS

# 2 does the branch exist on the remote?
/usr/bin/git -C "{{BASE}}" ls-remote --exit-code --heads origin \
  "feat/{{BRANCH_PREFIX}}-<id>-<slug>" && echo REMOTE_BRANCH

# 3 does it exist locally?
/usr/bin/git -C "{{BASE}}" rev-parse --verify --quiet \
  "refs/heads/feat/{{BRANCH_PREFIX}}-<id>-<slug>" && echo LOCAL_BRANCH
```

Then take EXACTLY ONE of three cases:

1. **`TREE_EXISTS`** → do NOT recreate it, do NOT create a second branch.
   RESUME in it. Read `{{BASE}}/runtime/handoff/night-<id>.md` — YOUR story's
   own handoff file, named for your queue id, never `night.md` and never another
   story's file — together with the PR body, for where the previous session
   stopped. A story session killed mid-flight must be able to continue.

   **That tree probably holds uncommitted work** — this case exists mostly
   because a previous session was killed mid-flight. BEFORE you read, build or
   change anything in it, run exactly this, and take exactly this action:

   ```bash
   /usr/bin/git -C "{{NIGHT_DIR}}/wt/<id>" status --porcelain
   ```

   - EMPTY → carry on normally.
   - NON-EMPTY → do exactly ONE thing with it: commit it as it stands, on the
     story branch, before any new work (one git command per Bash call):

     ```bash
     /usr/bin/git -C "{{NIGHT_DIR}}/wt/<id>" add -A
     /usr/bin/git -C "{{NIGHT_DIR}}/wt/<id>" -c user.name="$GIT_AUTHOR_NAME" -c user.email="$GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL" commit -m "WIP-RESUME <id>: inherited uncommitted work from a killed session [night {{RUN_DATE}}]"
     ```

     Then DECLARE it in BOTH the handoff and the PR body: one line with the
     commit SHA and the files in it, marked inherited, not written tonight. Do
     not stash it, do not remove the tree, do not discard it, and never present
     it as your story's work — shipping unexamined inherited changes silently is
     how a night merges work nobody can account for. If the review loop rejects
     it, revert it in a later commit and say so.
2. **No tree, but `REMOTE_BRANCH` or `LOCAL_BRANCH` printed** → attach it:

   ```bash
   /usr/bin/git -C "{{BASE}}" worktree add "{{NIGHT_DIR}}/wt/<id>" "feat/{{BRANCH_PREFIX}}-<id>-<slug>"
   ```

   Never invent a suffixed second branch (`-v2`, `-new`). Two branches for one
   story means two PRs and a merge conflict with yourself.
3. **None of the three checks printed anything** → create from the REMOTE base
   ref, never from whatever the local base branch happens to point at:

   ```bash
   /usr/bin/git -C "{{BASE}}" worktree add "{{NIGHT_DIR}}/wt/<id>" -b "feat/{{BRANCH_PREFIX}}-<id>-<slug>" "origin/{{BASE_BRANCH}}"
   ```

Immediately after creating a tree, append one tab-separated row to
`{{NIGHT_DIR}}/worktrees.tsv`:

    <id>	<path>	<branch>	<created ISO>

Morning cleanup is driven by that file: a tree missing from it is invisible to
cleanup, and a cleanup forced to pattern-match `$HOME` has already destroyed
uncommitted work once.

### 3.2 Git rules inside the worktree

- Work ONLY inside `{{NIGHT_DIR}}/wt/<id>`. One git command per Bash call, using
  the full path `/usr/bin/git`.
- **Never use bare `git stash` / `git stash pop`.** The stash stack is SHARED
  across every worktree of the repo and other sessions pop it concurrently — a
  bare `pop` can restore someone else's changes into your tree, or hand yours to
  them. Set work aside with a temporary WIP commit instead:

  ```bash
  /usr/bin/git -C "{{NIGHT_DIR}}/wt/<id>" add -A
  /usr/bin/git -C "{{NIGHT_DIR}}/wt/<id>" -c user.name="$GIT_AUTHOR_NAME" -c user.email="$GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL" commit -m "WIP: <what> [night <id>]"
  ```

  and amend or `git reset --soft HEAD~1` later. If a stash is genuinely
  unavoidable: `git stash push -u -m "night-<id>-<unique>"`, capture the SHA at
  once with `git stash list --format='%H %gs'`, restore with `git stash apply
  <sha>` (never `pop`), then drop that entry by re-finding it by tag.
- Never `rebase`, never `push --force`, never `reset --hard` onto a ref you did
  not just create.
- **Never remove, prune or force-remove ANY worktree — including your own.** Not
  `git worktree remove`, not `--force`, not `git worktree prune`, not `rm -rf`
  on a tree path. Cleanup belongs exclusively to the owner's morning phase,
  which first checks for unpushed commits and ignored files; a mid-run removal
  has already destroyed uncommitted work once. Leaving a directory on disk is
  always the cheaper mistake.
  The ONE exception is section 5.3 step 3: after YOUR OWN PR merged green and
  the `archive/` tag is VERIFIED on the remote, you remove YOUR OWN tree with
  a plain `git worktree remove` — never `--force`, never `prune`, never
  another story's tree, and never when a later queue entry names that tree.
- **Pin the commit identity on EVERY commit, from the ENVIRONMENT.** The runner
  exported this story's author identity — `{{GIT_USER_NAME}}`,
  `{{GIT_USER_EMAIL}}` — into your environment as `GIT_AUTHOR_NAME`,
  `GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL`, `GIT_COMMITTER_NAME`, `GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL`. Pass those
  variables, in DOUBLE quotes, so a freshly created worktree with no configured
  identity still commits:

  ```bash
  /usr/bin/git -C "{{NIGHT_DIR}}/wt/<id>" -c user.name="$GIT_AUTHOR_NAME" -c user.email="$GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL" commit -m "<message>"
  ```

  **Never retype the name into a shell command** — a real name can contain an
  apostrophe or `&`, and `-c user.name='O'Brien'` makes the command unparseable
  and every commit of this story fail. Use that form for the WIP commits above
  and for every real commit. Never run `git config --global` to work around it:
  that would change the identity of every other session on this shared machine.
- Commit messages in English, ending with the attribution line the repo's
  conventions prescribe.

### 3.3 Work

1. Consult the Brain before designing anything: `brain find <id>`,
   `brain predesign "<story title>"`. Honour every recorded decision it returns.
   Reopening a closed decision at night, with nobody to ask, is forbidden — if
   the story seems to require it, park with reason `review` and say why.
2. Read only the story's own backlog row and the documents that row or
   `brain predesign` points at. Delegate any read over 300 lines.
3. **TDD.** Write the failing test first, then the code that makes it pass. The
   review loop in section 4 checks for this, and a slice with no new test is a
   blocking finding.
4. Update the story's own backlog row in the same PR — honest coding percent,
   honest testing state. Never another story's row, never owner-only columns.
5. **One PR per story, and at most one open at a time**, into
   `{{BASE_BRANCH}}`: `gh pr create --base {{BASE_BRANCH}}`. PR body in English:
   what, why, evidence (test command and counts), non-blocking review findings,
   and what is left for the owner.

